{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqd5d1xatlo","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Judge epoch design decision: drop epoch cursor, switch to event triggered re-judgment","summary":"Decision: replace the epoch/cursor re-queue system in /judge/next with event-triggered re-judgment. Rationale: epoch design re-queues all ~600 edges every pass — including high-confidence edges already confirmed multiple times — causing a treadmill that never converges and wastes tokens on re-judgments with no new information.\n\nNew design:\n- /judge/next returns only: (a) weight=0 edges (new autowire, never judged) and (b) edges flagged needs_rejudge=true (endpoint changed)\n- When queue is empty → idle. No epoch flip, no re-queue.\n- Hooks in daemon state-change paths: task →canceled/superseded, note superseded → markForRejudge(nodeKey) finds all edges touching that node and sets needs_rejudge=true\n- Edge storage gains a needs_rejudge boolean flag\n- Migration: existing weight≥2 edges are stable, no re-queue; weight=0/1 edges stay in initial queue\n\nWhat stays the same: /judge/verdict, lib/judge.js RAG+agent logic, weight promotion on keep.\n\nWhy this over threshold filter: threshold filter keeps epochs (treadmill continues, just smaller); event-triggered gives true zero steady-state when graph is stable. Correct by design, not by approximation.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-14T02:10:18.238Z","vec":[-0.07455281913280487,0.015363230369985104,-0.03766107186675072,-0.005408376920968294,0.0004606854054145515,-0.04238323122262955,0.03542165830731392,-0.011481759138405323,0.027324918657541275,-0.011764350347220898,-0.014965521171689034,-0.0098967794328928,-0.005074252374470234,-0.06915706396102905,-0.08999940752983093,-0.0017518653767183423,0.11703146994113922,-0.009731393307447433,-0.03508506715297699,-0.06931398063898087,0.014926719479262829,-0.052535053342580795,0.01784966140985489,-0.004136558156460524,-0.102382592856884,-0.01313766185194254,-0.10725271701812